    "knowledge_point": "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor",
    "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{log}_{3}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[uv{|}_{1/2}^{1}-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x){|}_{1/2}^{1}-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})-{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-x{|}_{1/2}^{1}\\,] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0-(1-\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
