    "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-69726 follows Pascal's identity: binomial(5,3)=binomial(4,2)+binomial(4,3). q-69994 gives 4!/(2!2!)=6; q-70306 gives 5!/(2!3!)=10. q-88006 requires x>2 and ln(x-2)>=-6, hence x>=2+e^(-6)=(1+2e^6)/e^6. q-88037 requires x>1, whereas exponentiation gives x<=-1/(5e^2-1), so there is no solution. q-114551 uses integration by parts to obtain 3x^3 ln(x)-x^3+C. q-114679 evaluates [x ln(2x)-x]/ln(3) between 1/2 and 1, giving (2ln(2)-1)/(2ln(3)). q-115431 divides by the strictly positive 5^(x+6), giving 3x-1<8 and x<3. q-40368 has x^2+3>0, so its inequality reduces to x^2-9>=0. q-115673 has B=-7 and decomposition (2x-7)/(x^2+20)-1/(x+1). q-83161 has A=0 and decomposition 1/(x^2+4)+1/(x-1). q-115693 decomposes as (2x-3)/(x^2+3)+1/(x+4). q-49919 decomposes as 1/(x-1)-(x+1)/(x^2+1). Integrating these decompositions gives the selected logarithm and arctangent expressions; x/(2sqrt(5))=sqrt(5)x/10 and x/sqrt(3)=sqrt(3)x/3 explain the displayed arctangent forms. q-3617 decomposes as 2/(x-1)+(x+2)/(x^2+4x-1); the second numerator is half the denominator's derivative. q-3638 decomposes as (10x-15)/(2x^2-6x+9)-5/(x-4); the first numerator is 5/2 times the quadratic's derivative, and that quadratic is always positive. q-14770 uses the inverse derivative formula: f'(0)=4, hence (f^(-1))'(10)=1/4. Each answer has direct worked-solution evidence; no source grade or earlier submitted answer was supplied.",
